  <title>5.4.5: Interpret the Growth Factor of the Equation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01293</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.4.5: Interpret the Growth Factor of the Equation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f6b31f4e-0b6f-42ab-91b2-e203d4753048</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (15 minutes)</title>
<para>A group of biologists is surveying the mice population in a forest. The equation \(n=75\cdot 3^t\) gives the total number of mice, \(n\), \(t\) years since the survey began. Explain what the numbers 75 and 3 mean in this situation.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 75 is the initial number of mice in the forest when the survey started, or the number of mice when \(t\) is 0. The 3 is the growth factor or constant ratio, meaning that each year, the population of mice in the forest is 3 times the previous year’s population.</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>Students will have more opportunities to understand the mathematical ideas in this cool-down, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool-down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize over the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.</para></section>
